    "4.2 Write property test for credential encryption round-trip": {
      "status": "failed",
      "failingExample": "test_tampered_ciphertext_raises_decryption_error failed.\nFalsifying example: plaintext='', data draws -> index=0, replacement byte such that the mutated base64 character decodes to the same token bits (Draw 1: 97, Draw 2: 97).\n\nAssertionError: tampered ciphertext was accepted by decrypt()\n\nTriage: This is a TEST-STRATEGY flaw, not a code bug. A Fernet token is urlsafe-base64-encoded TEXT. Mutating a single byte of the base64 representation does not guarantee a different underlying token: some base64 characters decode to the same 6-bit group (and trailing/padding bits are ignored), so the decoded token is unchanged and still authenticates. The Encryption_Service correctly uses authenticated encryption; the other 6 properties (text/bytes round-trip, opaque ciphertext, nondeterministic re-encryption, truncation raises DecryptionError) all pass. To correctly express Req 6.3 tamper-detection, the test should base64-decode the token, flip a byte in the RAW token bytes, re-encode, then assert DecryptionError (or mutate and assert \"either equal to original OR raises DecryptionError\").",
      "lastRunTimestamp": 1788877759487
    },
